WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.949
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Jamal Greene, who teaches constitutional

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:10.300
law at Columbia University and also is author
of How Rights Went Wrong Why Our Obsession

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.230
with Rights is Tearing America Apart.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.230
It's so great to have you on.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:15.269
I appreciate it.

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:16.390
Good to be here.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:17.390
Thanks.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:20.490
So maybe just to frame our conversation, you
know, one of the things I've talked about

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:26.840
over the last few years is that a lot of these
movements that claim to be or are ostensibly

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:32.200
about rights and about staking a claim to
a right, exercising a certain right, they

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.800
seem to do this in the absence of an understanding
that with rights come responsibilities and

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:44.469
that the claiming of rights in a vacuum seems
to be untenable and in some cases an impossibility

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:46.670
sort of in modern modern society.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.750
I'd love to kind of first hear your thoughts
a little bit about that and the idea of our

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:57.489
rights, something that we should be exploring
in a vacuum absent any cultural societal reality,

00:00:57.489 --> 00:01:00.610
or is that context absolutely crucial to thinking
about rights?

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:05.140
Speaker 5: Well, I certainly think that rights
are embedded within a society.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:12.470
And and we actually have a very long tradition
in the United States of thinking about rights

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:17.110
and their limits as something that is a collective
exercise.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.189
That's actually how the framers thought about
rights.

00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:24.990
But we've gone away from that vision and gotten
to a place where and I think this is where

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.249
your political movements come in.

00:01:27.249 --> 00:01:33.679
We tend to to use rights as political leverage
because we tend to think about them in almost

00:01:33.679 --> 00:01:38.530
absolute terms as as you say, in a vacuum,
kind of disconnected from all else, that once

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.820
you claim a right, then you get to win.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:41.820
Right.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.819
So the whole game becomes, can I claim a right?

00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:51.490
Can I get a relevant decision makers sometimes
courts to to say that I have a right.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:54.349
And that's what's going to win me my political
battle.

00:01:54.349 --> 00:01:59.639
And we I think we need to get back to thinking
about rights in political terms, which is

00:01:59.639 --> 00:02:03.100
to say in terms of of being part of a society.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:09.399
Speaker 1: What is a framework where we can
think about situations where rights seemingly

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:16.700
conflict or when we're talking about one's
freedom or rights to do something versus someone

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.920
else's freedom or a right to be free of that
something?

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.290
What's the framework that we should be applying
to those conflicts?

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.950
Speaker 5: Well, one of the things I emphasized
in the book is that rights inherently conflict.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.210
They always conflict.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:36.131
And you can if you think about your right
to form a society and to pass laws, right

00:02:36.131 --> 00:02:39.810
to government is designed to protect rights.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.900
And there's also, of course, another side
where there's individuals who are claiming

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.819
particular rights against what government
does.

00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:46.220
So there's constantly conflict.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:49.900
You've got to have a strategy for managing
that conflict.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:55.609
In the US, our legal system has developed
to a place where the way we live, the way

00:02:55.609 --> 00:02:59.840
we manage it, is to kind of pretend it doesn't
exist, pretend the conflict isn't there and

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.910
that one side is right and the other side
is wrong.

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:06.540
So you've got to pick who has the right in
any particular case, whereas in actually many

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.340
other countries, in fact, most other countries,
the way in which the legal system tends to

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.620
respond is through.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.010
I'll use a legalistic term called proportionality.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:21.579
But what it really means is that you kind
of background the question of who has the

00:03:21.579 --> 00:03:22.579
right.

00:03:22.579 --> 00:03:25.629
It just assumed that rights exist on both
sides of a lot of legal questions and you

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:30.740
focus much more heavily on what is the government
doing, what kind of problem is it trying to

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:31.939
respond to?

00:03:31.939 --> 00:03:34.329
What kind of evidence is it relying on?

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:36.579
What kind of alternatives are available to
it?

00:03:36.579 --> 00:03:40.959
Is it acting totally disproportionately or
is it acting basically reasonably?

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:45.519
Those are the kinds of questions that we typically
ask when we're asking about if this just is

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:46.610
this fair.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:50.769
And those questions are totally consistent
with the notion that that there are rights

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:56.290
involved, because you've got a what I say
in the book is mediate between different claims

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:01.920
of rights on multiple sides of a conflict
and get really factual questions of, well,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.450
what's what's actually going on here.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:08.689
We don't need to to get into the kind of legalistic
questions of what is the First Amendment mean

00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:11.279
or what does the Constitution say.

00:04:11.279 --> 00:04:15.609
Those questions don't get us very far in answering
questions about rights conflicts.

00:04:15.609 --> 00:04:18.019
We've got to get into the into the factual
details.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:21.940
Speaker 1: So, I mean, to take a case, maybe
many in my audience will be familiar with

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:27.400
this infamous gay wedding cake case from from
the state of Colorado.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:34.190
One interpretation of that would be that it's
a question of the right of a business owner

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:39.419
to make decisions about how they want to operate
their business and the right of the couple

00:04:39.419 --> 00:04:44.690
wanting the cake not to be discriminated against
on the basis of sexual orientation.

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:47.820
It might it could be framed as a right versus
a right.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:54.000
All sorts of different participants have reframed
it where there's other ways to think about

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.000
it.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.980
One could say, well, it's a judge or jury
choosing as to which whose rights reigns supreme.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:01.479
But is it not that simple?

00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:05.580
Speaker 5: I think it's not that simple and
it's a it's a fascinating case because I think

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.840
it really does kind of burrow down on this
on this problem where I actually think there's

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.830
even more than two sets of rights involved.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:18.520
So there's the rights of a baker who wants
to to decide which which wedding cakes for.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:19.520
Right.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:21.620
And he's claiming a religious right and also
a free speech.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:22.620
Right.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.620
Because his cakes are really artistic.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:24.620
So.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:25.620
Right.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:26.620
He thinks that it's a way of speaking.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:29.720
There's the right of the couple to to not
be discriminated against, as you say, David.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:34.470
And then there's the right of the people of
Colorado passed a law that says don't discriminate

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:35.870
on the basis of sexual orientation.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:40.110
So they've got rights to at least the way
I frame it.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.990
So there's rights on all sides.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:48.121
And you could think of it in the most kind
of binary terms as a judge or jury has got

00:05:48.121 --> 00:05:50.580
to pick, you know, whose rights count.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:59.070
But there actually are lot, lots of overlap
in how each of the sides in that case understood

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:00.960
the rights of the other side.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:08.590
So the couple actually believed that a baker
can choose whether to bake a cake for someone

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:10.530
he ideologically disagrees with.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.039
He can decide not to bake a cake.

00:06:12.039 --> 00:06:13.370
And the couple agreed with that.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:19.169
The baker agreed that he actually can't discriminate
on the basis of sexual orientation in baking

00:06:19.169 --> 00:06:20.169
his cakes.

00:06:20.169 --> 00:06:23.110
He didn't claim a First Amendment right to
avoid discrimination.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:27.900
What the disagreement was, was a much more
narrow disagreement about whether refusing

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:35.030
to bake a cake for a same sex wedding counted
as discrimination on the basis of sexual orientation.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:39.460
Because the baker said, I don't care who my
customers are, I just don't want to participate

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:40.990
in this wedding.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:43.300
So that's an ideological dispute.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.159
According to him, he's not actually discriminating
against any people.

00:06:46.159 --> 00:06:50.590
And the couple said, well, no, if this had
been an opposite sex wedding, you would have

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:51.590
you would have baked the cake.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:52.710
And so that is discrimination.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.750
But that's actually a pretty narrow conflict.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:07:01.070
And what I'm trying to suggest in the book
is that judges really need to focus on on

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:06.520
the narrow part of the conflict rather than
trying to decide, you know, do we care more

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:10.689
about religious freedom or do we care more
about equality for same sex couples?

00:07:10.689 --> 00:07:14.560
Those are values, conflicts that we're not
going to resolve in a court case and shouldn't

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:15.560
try to resolve.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:19.730
But we can actually get much closer together
if we focus on individual details.

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:24.610
Speaker 1: In fact, I guess that's the tricky
thing, where it's very common that you will

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:31.720
have a case that starts with what appears
to be a decision or a conflict between some

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:36.740
broader values about how society or an economy
or a culture should operate.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:42.180
And by the time it gets to either the initial
court decision or even on an appeal, some

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.889
subsequent court's decision, it ends up being
something very, very narrow.

00:07:46.889 --> 00:07:51.789
And I think to some degree, that is a source
of frustration for many people who would like

00:07:51.789 --> 00:07:55.849
to have these broader issues resolved, sort
of like once and for all.

00:07:55.849 --> 00:08:00.450
And it doesn't happen because the courts end
up very narrowly making making a decision,

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:01.509
as you point out.

00:08:01.509 --> 00:08:05.009
How should the broader questions be dealt
with?

00:08:05.009 --> 00:08:10.360
Because when they're dealt with so-called
democratically, either by by, you know, a

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:17.729
ballot initiative or by the legislatures that
we elect often, it's still not permanent in

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:20.860
that then it's undone at the next opportunity.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:23.870
I mean, how should a society deal with the
broader question?

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:28.669
Speaker 5: Well, the broader questions of
values, conflicts, I think the first thing

00:08:28.669 --> 00:08:31.229
I'll say is they shouldn't be resolved by
courts.

00:08:31.229 --> 00:08:38.040
OK, and this is the this is it writes, conflicts
are ultimately political conflicts or conflicts

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:41.150
between people who have fundamental disagreements
with one another.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:43.710
And it's OK to have fundamental disagreements
with one another.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:45.600
That's what makes us human.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:51.350
And in some ways, the core of the book is
to say, look, the reason we disagree about

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:56.390
rights is not because one of us is right and
the other is because some of us are angels

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:59.160
and others devils, because we're different
people.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.480
We have different values, different commitments.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:07.090
And when you when you overlay a legal system
onto that kind of disagreement, the legal

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:13.280
system has to figure out how do you construct
rules that enable people to live together

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.900
and to stay invested within the constitutional
system.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:20.530
So we actually celebrate uncertainty, at least
to a degree.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:26.050
I celebrate not resolving rights conflicts
for all time because that that that means

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:30.900
that the Constitution doesn't protect or doesn't
care about one or the other side of really

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.570
important values, conflicts.

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:35.150
I don't think that's the way a legal system
should operate.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:38.570
I think you try to mediate those conflicts
through politics.

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:42.690
And when I say politics, I don't always mean
legislative politics or ballot initiatives.

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:44.880
I mean other kinds of institutions.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.190
Journalists are are engaged in acts of politics.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:53.690
When we go to when we have social media conversation,
that's politics.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:56.380
Schools are engaged in forms of politics.

00:09:56.380 --> 00:10:05.340
Politics is is is is is constructing a political
culture, a culture of understanding what the

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:09.750
core of our disagreements might be, understanding
the perspective of other sides and trying

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:11.780
to work towards a solution.

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:15.930
And that's what that's what political negotiation
looks like.

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:20.450
And that that again, that doesn't necessarily
happen in legislatures, although that's the

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:27.810
sort of ultimate that's the ultimate, you
know, power wielder is the legislature.

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:31.460
But before you get to the legislature, there's
a whole lot of political conversation that

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.090
has to happen that gets shut off.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:37.840
If we think that the way to resolve our political
conflicts is to give them to courts who are

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.330
going to make kind of legalistic, abstract
decisions that don't get us very far to addressing

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:43.330
our conflicts.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:47.460
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to ask about,
you know, during the run up to the 2020 election,

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:51.450
I received a lot of emails from viewers and
listeners, particularly in Europe, who said,

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:56.130
you know, I'm following this election in the
United States and I'm struck by how much talk

00:10:56.130 --> 00:11:01.440
there is of people asserting rights, the discussion
of my right to free speech, my right to vote,

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:07.130
my right to a gun, et cetera, saying that
in in their countries, this idea of asserting

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:10.800
rights just is not a it's not a narrative
in elections.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:15.090
And that they found that there was they were
curious about like, how did this become such

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:19.000
a thing in American politics where people
are constantly talking about rights and asserting

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:20.000
rights?

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.050
Is there some particular history to that in
the United States that we can point to about

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:28.740
how this issue of the asserting of rights
became such a political discussion that in

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:30.610
other countries it simply is not so?

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:32.870
Speaker 5: So I'll say a couple of things.

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:38.560
One is, is that in Europe, they do care about
rights, but they just don't necessarily think

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:42.390
of rights as kind of trumps that kind of overcome
everything else.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:45.520
And that's what's really, I think, different
about our political culture.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:50.370
And I think that I try to tell the story in
the in the book and kind of the first third

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:51.940
of the book.

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:54.810
It doesn't come from the founding, which is
what we often tell ourselves.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:12:01.080
The founders had a much more communitarian
view of rights than than modern Americans

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:02.080
do.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:04.990
It really comes out of the 1960s in the post
civil rights era legacy.

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:08.000
And it's a it's a it's a complex story.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:15.150
But what it boils down to is, is a history
with race and with the particular pathologies,

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:20.390
the particular problem of of people who are
being racially discriminated against in overt

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:27.870
ways, being kind of the paradigm of rights
in America, right to the core right is the

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:29.990
right against racial discrimination.

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:31.670
And that's not something you mediate.

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:35.430
It's not something where you say, well, you
know, let's see what the segregationists think

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.860
and then we'll see what other people think.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:42.530
It doesn't lend itself to mediation because
one side of that dispute is completely bad

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:47.090
faith, the oppressive, abusive actor.

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:52.260
But you can't associate all of government
with you know, any time I write is at stake.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:55.060
That means the government's being abusive
or oppressive.

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:56.530
That's not most rights.

00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:57.880
That's not most rights conflicts.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:02.950
And so I think we need a language in which
we can talk about ordinary political conflict

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:08.320
that doesn't take the form of this is let's
see if this is just like Jim Crow or let's

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:09.520
see if this is just like McCarthyism.

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:14.390
We've got to take rights conflicts on their
facts and actually think about what the government's

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:18.470
trying to accomplish and whether it's something
that is considered to be legitimate within

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:19.470
our tradition.

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:24.340
Speaker 1: Very of very well said the book,
as we've been mentioning.

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:29.750
Is how rights went wrong, why our obsession
with rights is tearing America apart.

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:34.200
We've been talking with the author of the
book, Professor Jamal Greene.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:35.200
It's so great to have you on.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:36.490
I really appreciate your time.

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:37.681
Speaker 5: Great to be here.

00:13:37.681 --> 00:13:38.681
I enjoyed the conversation.

00:13:38.681 --> 00:13:39.681
Thanks.

